WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.640
A rack choice starts with what the deployment must do.

2
00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:07.520
Specify the equipment population and its expected operating envelope, rather than choosing

3
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.400
a frame and hoping the workload fits.

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.740
Sustained demand, short excursions, cooling conditions and acceptable behavior during a fault

5
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.260
can lead to different designs even at the same advertised rack power.

6
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.480
Next, identify the constraints that equipment creates.

7
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.700
Weight and center of gravity affect support and handling.

8
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.640
Cable and hose routes affect installation and service.

9
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.060
Heat that isn't captured by liquid still needs an air path.

10
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:35.660
A maintenance strategy may require access that an otherwise compatible layout obstructs.

11
00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.860
Turn these needs into a requirements sheet with measurable conditions and an

12
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.680
owner for every unresolved item.

13
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.240
For example, a cooling requirement should identify the permitted operating conditions and

14
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.520
the interface where they're guaranteed, not just say liquid cooled.

15
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.220
Keep the initial design envelope explicit.

16
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.760
Later modules will use it to distinguish a product that looks suitable

17
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.880
from a deployment with enough evidence to proceed.

